WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.138
This slide introduces a pattern that connects SQL
querying with data preparation

00:00:04.162 --> 00:00:10.112
workflows INSERT INTO followed by SELECT The idea is
simple First

00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:14.888
a SELECT query identifies a set of rows Instead of
only displaying

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:18.888
those rows INSERT INTO SELECT can copy the selected
values into another

00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:24.362
table The example first creates a table called
senior_patients with patient ID

00:00:24.525 --> 00:00:29.225
MRN full name and age Then the INSERT statement
names the target

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.850
columns The SELECT portion retrieves those same
fields from patients where age

00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:38.900
is greater than or equal to sixty-five Finally
another SELECT displays the

00:00:38.912 --> 00:00:43.362
derived table Run the example and follow the process
in three stages

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.450
create the destination populate it from a query and
inspect the result

00:00:48.338 --> 00:00:53.350
This pattern appears in staging workflows ETL or ELT
processes archives reporting

00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:57.212
tables and data marts It can be very useful when you
need

00:00:57.225 --> 00:01:02.362
to move or reshape selected data inside a database
One important detail

00:01:02.375 --> 00:01:05.812
is that the SELECT output must be compatible with
the target columns

00:01:06.688 --> 00:01:09.850
The number of values their order and their data
types should make

00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:14.362
sense for the destination table For now think of
INSERT INTO SELECT

00:01:14.375 --> 00:01:16.712
as taking the result of a query and using it as the

00:01:16.738 --> 00:01:18.062
input to another table
